## Why This Is Asked
Tests ability to design a concrete, methodical debugging approach that isolates performance bottlenecks across a CNPA stack under load, balancing observability needs with minimal production impact.

## Key Concepts
- Request correlation through end-to-end request_id propagation
- Per-stage timing granularity for bottleneck isolation
- Targeted metrics: p95/p99 latency, throughput, queue depths
- Instrumentation efficiency: lightweight traces, strategic sampling
- Data-driven verification of optimization impact

## Code Example
```javascript
// Add per-stage timing with request correlation in Node.js HTTP handler
function handleRequest(req, res) {
  const requestId = req.headers['x-request-id'] ?? generateId();
  const startTime = process.hrtime.bigint();

  // HTTP processing timer
  const httpStart = process.hrtime.bigint();
  // ... request parsing and validation
  const httpDuration = Number(process.hrtime.bigint() - httpStart) / 1000000;

  // Database operation timer
  const dbStart = process.hrtime.bigint();
  await database.save(data);
  const dbDuration = Number(process.hrtime.bigint() - dbStart) / 1000000;

  // Kafka publishing timer
  const kafkaStart = process.hrtime.bigint();
  await kafkaProducer.send(event);
  const kafkaDuration = Number(process.hrtime.bigint() - kafkaStart) / 1000000;

  // Log metrics with request correlation
  metrics.record('http_latency', httpDuration, { requestId });
  metrics.record('db_latency', dbDuration, { requestId });
  metrics.record('kafka_latency', kafkaDuration, { requestId });
}
```

## Why This Is Asked
This question assesses the ability to design a lightweight, beginner-friendly tracing path that minimizes overhead while providing end-to-end visibility across CNPA components.

## Key Concepts
- Lightweight, opt-in tracing to avoid Always-On overhead
- Sampling strategies (1%) to bound performance impact
- Data minimization and PII redaction for compliance
- Asynchronous persistence to prevent blocking request handling
- Storage choice between Postgres (structured queries) vs Redis (high throughput, shorter retention)
- Observability integration and performance regression testing

## Code Example
```javascript
// Pseudo-code: activate trace if header is set, then persist asynchronously
function traceMiddleware(req, res, next) {
  if (req.headers['x-trace'] !== 'true') return next();

  const shouldSample = Math.random() < 0.01; // 1% sampling
  if (!shouldSample) return next();

  const traceData = {
    request_id: generateUUID(),
    timestamp: new Date().toISOString(),
    user_id: redactPii(req.user?.id), // PII redaction
    action: req.path,
    db_pointer: null
  };

  // Async persist - non-blocking
  persistTrace(traceData).catch(console.error);

  next();
}

async function persistTrace(data) {
  await db.query(
    'INSERT INTO traces (request_id, timestamp, user_id, action) VALUES ($1, $2, $3, $4)',
    [data.request_id, data.timestamp, data.user_id, data.action]
  );
}
```

## Why This Is Asked
Tests understanding of cross-layer backpressure and tenant isolation in CNPA. Requires concrete knobs, metrics, rollout strategy, and rollback; demonstrates awareness of end-to-end latency, SLOs, and risk controls.

## Key Concepts
- Per-tenant isolation
- Backpressure and rate limiting
- Canary/feature flag rollout
- Observability across HTTP, DB, Kafka, Redis
- Rollback and safety nets

## Code Example
```javascript
// Pseudo per-tenant rate limiter sketch
class TenantLimiter {
  constructor() {
    this.store = new Map();
  }
  acquire(tenantId) {
    let bucket = this.store.get(tenantId);
    if (!bucket) {
      bucket = new TokenBucket(tenantId, 100, 1); // rate, burst
      this.store.set(tenantId, bucket);
    }
    if (!bucket.tryConsume()) {
      throw new Error("TooManyRequests");
    }
    return true;
  }
}
```

